<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- THE SYSTEM SPLASH, THEMED WITH OUR OWN PICTURE — AND THIS IS HOW AN APP "REPLACES" IT.

     From Android 12 (API 31) the platform draws a splash for every app: the launcher icon on a background
     colour, shown during the window transition while the process is created. Until now this app did not opt in,
     so the phone drew ITS OWN icon splash and then our `launch_background` drawable — TWO different pictures,
     from two different systems, one after the other. That is the seam, and it cannot be removed by deleting
     anything: the window transition happens before Dart exists.

     WHAT CAN BE DONE, and is: give the platform OUR picture, so the screen it shows is the same screen our
     boot screen draws. `windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon` + `windowSplashScreenBackground` are the two that
     matter. Attributes verified present in this machine's `android-35/android.jar` rather than written from
     memory.

     ONE CONSTRAINT SHAPES THE DESIGN: the platform MASKS this icon to a circle of roughly 160dp (the launcher
     icon's outer 2/3), so a wide or tall picture is CROPPED, not scaled. That is why every app you have seen
     does this with its icon alone — and it is the reason the splash asset is now the mark by itself, with the
     artwork inside the safe circle. A wordmark would be cut in half.

     AND `postSplashScreenTheme` IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT. It is not a platform attribute — checked, it is not in
     `android.R$attr` on API 35 — it belongs to the AndroidX `core-splashscreen` library, which this project
     does not use and does not need: without it the window simply keeps this theme until Flutter's first frame,
     and Flutter switches to `NormalTheme` through the manifest's `NormalTheme` meta-data.

     `android:windowBackground` is kept alongside the splash attributes. It is the PRE-31 path: on Android 11
     and below there is no system splash, so the drawable IS the launch screen. Both routes now show the same
     picture.

     values-night IS THE SAME FILE. The app is dark on every device, so there is no light variant to disagree
     with. -->
<resources>
    <style name="LaunchTheme" parent="@android:style/Theme.Black.NoTitleBar">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@drawable/launch_background</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">false</item>
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@color/splash_background</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@color/splash_background</item>

        <!-- THE SYSTEM SPLASH (API 31+). Ours, not the launcher icon. -->
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/splash_background</item>
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@drawable/splash_logo</item>
        <!-- No icon backdrop: our mark is the colour, and a plate behind it would show as a square in the
             middle of the circle the platform masks to. -->
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenIconBackgroundColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <!-- 0, so the platform hands over to Flutter the moment the engine is ready. Any value here is an
             artificial hold in front of the first frame, which is the opposite of what the boot screen is for. -->
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenAnimationDuration">0</item>
    </style>
    <style name="NormalTheme" parent="@android:style/Theme.Black.NoTitleBar">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/splash_background</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">false</item>
    </style>
</resources>
